module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.TrivSqZeroExt.Ideal
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.VectorSpace
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.LocalCriteria
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.Nilpotent
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Flatness over an Artin local ring, and over an arbitrary square-zero extension

This file records the criteria for flatness of a module over a square-zero or nilpotent thickening
that the thickened ring being *local* makes unconditional, in the sense that the auxiliary
flatness hypothesis the general criteria carry is discharged here rather than assumed.

The general criteria are already stated for an arbitrary ring and an arbitrary ideal:
`flat_of_sq_zero_local_criterion` for a square-zero ideal, and
`Module.Flat.iff_flat_quotient_and_isZero_Tor'_one` ([Stacks, Tag 051K]) for a proper ideal over a
local ring with nilpotent maximal ideal. Besides a Tor-type condition both ask that the reduction
`M/JM` be flat over `A/J`. When `J` is *maximal* the ring `A/J` is a field, over which every
module is flat, so that hypothesis disappears and a single condition remains:

* `Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal`: for `J` maximal with `J ^ 2 = 0`, an `A`-module
  `M` is flat exactly when the multiplication map `J ⊗[A] M → M` is injective;
* `Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_maximalIdeal`: the same for `J` the maximal ideal of a local
  ring, which is the form a base `Spec A` with `A` Artinian local presents;
* `Module.Flat.iff_isZero_Tor'_one_residueField_of_isNilpotent`: if the maximal ideal of a local
  ring is merely nilpotent then a module is flat exactly when `Tor₁^A(κ, M)` vanishes, for `κ` the
  residue field.

Note that maximality of `J` is not an extra demand on top of square-zeroness in the intended
application: an ideal `J` with `J ^ 2 = 0` consists of nilpotents, so it is contained in every
maximal ideal, and if it is itself maximal then it is the unique one, i.e. `A` is local with
maximal ideal `J`. Stating the criterion with `J.IsMaximal` rather than with
`[IsLocalRing A]` and `J = 𝔪` avoids having to produce the `IsLocalRing` instance at a use site
where the quotient is visibly a field, which is what
`Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_kerIdeal` below exploits.

## Why this generalizes the trivial square-zero extension

`Deformation.Examples.flat_realization` proves flatness of the middle ring of a square-zero
extension over the *trivial* square-zero extension `k ⊕ V` of a field `k`, that is over
`TrivSqZeroExt k V`. Its augmentation ideal `TrivSqZeroExt.kerIdeal k V` is maximal
(`TrivSqZeroExt.isMaximal_kerIdeal`) and squares to zero (`TrivSqZeroExt.kerIdeal_sq`), so that
base is one instance of the criterion here; `Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_kerIdeal` is the
resulting statement, and it is what makes "generalizes" a theorem rather than a remark.

A square-zero extension of a field need not split. `ZMod 4` is local, its maximal ideal is
generated by `2` and squares to zero, and `ZMod 4` is not `TrivSqZeroExt k V` for any field `k`
and `k`-vector space `V`: such a ring has `k` as a subring, so its characteristic is `0` or prime,
never `4`. Those non-split bases are the ones mixed-characteristic deformation theory runs over,
and they are covered by `Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal` and not by
`Deformation.Examples.flat_realization`.

The generalization is also a strengthening at the split base itself.
`Deformation.Examples.flat_realization` goes through `Ideal.bijective_tensorSMulHomToKer_iff`
([Stacks, Tag 063Y], affine case), which asks the canonical conormal map to be *bijective* and
asks separately that the reduction be flat over the quotient ring. Over a maximal square-zero
ideal neither extra demand is needed: injectivity alone suffices, because the quotient the
criterion reduces along is a field.

Flatness over a non-reduced base is genuinely restrictive and none of this trivializes it. Over
`A = k[x]/x²` the residue field `k` is not a flat `A`-module, and correspondingly
`𝔪 ⊗[A] k → k` is the zero map on a one-dimensional space, so it is not injective.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 051C](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/051C)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 051K](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/051K)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 063Y](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063Y)
-/

@[expose] public section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits TensorProduct

universe u v

namespace TrivSqZeroExt

/-- **The augmentation ideal of the trivial square-zero extension of a field is maximal.** The
augmentation `k ⊕ V → k` is a surjection onto a field with this ideal as its kernel.

Together with `TrivSqZeroExt.kerIdeal_sq` this exhibits `TrivSqZeroExt k V` as a ring with a
maximal square-zero ideal, so the flatness criterion
`Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal` applies to it. -/
@[ega "IV 11.7.1" "partial"]
theorem isMaximal_kerIdeal (k : Type u) [Field k] (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V] [Module k V]
    [Module kᵐᵒᵖ V] [IsCentralScalar k V] : (kerIdeal k V).IsMaximal :=
  RingHom.ker_isMaximal_of_surjective (fstHom k k V) fun c => ⟨inl c, fst_inl V c⟩

end TrivSqZeroExt

namespace Module.Flat

/-- **Every module over the quotient by a maximal ideal is flat**: that quotient is a field, so
the module is free.

This is the auxiliary hypothesis that the general flatness criteria carry and that taking the
ideal to be maximal discharges. -/
theorem of_quotient_isMaximal {A : Type u} [CommRing A] (J : Ideal A) [J.IsMaximal]
    (N : Type v) [AddCommGroup N] [Module (A ⧸ J) N] :
    Module.Flat (A ⧸ J) N := by
  let := Ideal.Quotient.field J
  exact Module.Flat.of_free

/-- The multiplication map `J ⊗[A] M → M` of an ideal, written as the inclusion of `J` tensored
with `M` followed by the identification `A ⊗[A] M ≃ M`. The left-hand side is the spelling
`flat_of_sq_zero_local_criterion` uses and the right-hand side is the one the flatness criteria
of Mathlib speak about. -/
theorem lift_lsmul_eq_lid_comp_rTensor {A : Type u} [CommRing A] (J : Ideal A)
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] :
    TensorProduct.lift ((LinearMap.lsmul A M).comp J.subtype) =
      (TensorProduct.lid A M).toLinearMap ∘ₗ LinearMap.rTensor M J.subtype := by
  ext x m
  simp

/-- **Flatness over a maximal square-zero ideal.** Let `J` be a maximal ideal of `A` with
`J ^ 2 = 0`. Then an `A`-module `M` is flat exactly when the multiplication map `J ⊗[A] M → M` is
injective.

No flatness hypothesis on the reduction `M/JM` appears: it is a module over the field `A/J`, so it
is flat by `Module.Flat.of_quotient_isMaximal`. This is the generalization of
`Deformation.Examples.flat_realization` from the trivial square-zero extension `k ⊕ V` of a field
to an arbitrary square-zero extension; see the module docstring. -/
theorem iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal {A : Type u} [CommRing A] (J : Ideal A) [J.IsMaximal]
    (hJ : J ^ 2 = ⊥) (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] :
    Module.Flat A M ↔
      Function.Injective (TensorProduct.lift ((LinearMap.lsmul A M).comp J.subtype)) := by
  refine ⟨fun h => ?_, fun hinj =>
    flat_of_sq_zero_local_criterion J hJ (of_quotient_isMaximal J _) hinj⟩
  rw [lift_lsmul_eq_lid_comp_rTensor]
  exact (TensorProduct.lid A M).injective.comp
    (Module.Flat.rTensor_preserves_injective_linearMap (M := M) _
      (Submodule.injective_subtype _))

/-- **Flatness over a local ring with square-zero maximal ideal.** This is
`Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal` for `J` the maximal ideal, which is the form a base
`Spec A` with `A` Artinian local presents. -/
theorem iff_injective_lsmul_maximalIdeal {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    (hm : IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A ^ 2 = ⊥)
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] :
    Module.Flat A M ↔
      Function.Injective (TensorProduct.lift
        ((LinearMap.lsmul A M).comp (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A).subtype)) :=
  have := IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal.isMaximal A
  iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) hm M

/-- **Flatness over the trivial square-zero extension of a field.** A module over `k ⊕ V` is flat
exactly when the multiplication map `𝔪 ⊗ M → M` of its augmentation ideal is injective.

This is the base of `Deformation.Examples.flat_realization`, and the point of recording the
specialization is that it is deduced from `Module.Flat.iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal` with no
hypothesis on `V` and nothing specific to the splitting: the only inputs are
`TrivSqZeroExt.isMaximal_kerIdeal` and `TrivSqZeroExt.kerIdeal_sq`. -/
theorem iff_injective_lsmul_kerIdeal (k : Type u) [Field k] (V : Type u) [AddCommGroup V]
    [Module k V] [Module kᵐᵒᵖ V] [IsCentralScalar k V]
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module (TrivSqZeroExt k V) M] :
    Module.Flat (TrivSqZeroExt k V) M ↔
      Function.Injective (TensorProduct.lift
        ((LinearMap.lsmul (TrivSqZeroExt k V) M).comp (TrivSqZeroExt.kerIdeal k V).subtype)) :=
  have := TrivSqZeroExt.isMaximal_kerIdeal k V
  iff_injective_lsmul_of_isMaximal (TrivSqZeroExt.kerIdeal k V)
    (TrivSqZeroExt.kerIdeal_sq k V) M

/-- **The local criterion of flatness over an Artin local ring.** Over a local ring whose maximal
ideal is nilpotent — an Artinian local ring, for instance — a module is flat exactly when `Tor₁`
against the residue field vanishes. Here `Tor₁^A(κ, M)` is
`((Tor' (ModuleCat A) 1).obj κ).obj M`, the first left derived functor of `- ⊗[A] M` at `κ`.

This is [Stacks, Tag 051K] with the ideal taken to be the maximal ideal, which discharges that
statement's flatness hypothesis on the reduction by `Module.Flat.of_quotient_isMaximal`. Unlike
`Module.Flat.of_isZero_Tor'_one_residueField` ([Stacks, Tag 00MK]), which reaches the same
conclusion for a Noetherian local base and a finite module, nothing here is Noetherian and the
module is arbitrary; what replaces those hypotheses is nilpotence of the maximal ideal. -/
@[ega "0 10.1.3" "(i): c ⇔ d, c ⇔ d",
  ega "0 10.2.1" "special case",
  ega "0 10.2.2" "special case",
  ega "IV 11.2.5" "special case",
  ega "IV 11.3.12" "special case",
  sga "1 IV 4.3" "case (a): (iii) ⇔ (iv)",
  sga "1 IV 5.5" "(i)⇔(ii), special case",
  sga "1 IV 5.6" "(i)⇔(ii), special case"]
theorem iff_isZero_Tor'_one_residueField_of_isNilpotent {A : Type u} [CommRing A] [IsLocalRing A]
    (hnil : IsNilpotent (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A))
    (M : Type u) [AddCommGroup M] [Module A M] :
    Module.Flat A M ↔
      IsZero (((Tor' (ModuleCat.{u} A) 1).obj
        (ModuleCat.of A (A ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A))).obj (ModuleCat.of A M)) := by
  have := IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal.isMaximal A
  rw [iff_flat_quotient_and_isZero_Tor'_one M (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal A) hnil
    (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal.isMaximal A).ne_top]
  exact ⟨fun h => h.2, fun h => ⟨of_quotient_isMaximal _ _, h⟩⟩

end Module.Flat

end
